  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAnother rinse-repeat forecast for the upcoming period, as no\nsignificant change in the overall synoptic pattern has occurred\nnor is forecast to occur: The forecast continues to be very \nstraight- forward through the next week, and confidence is \ntherefore high in the forecast details. Global model ensembles \nagree that an anomalously warm upper level ridge will slowly shift\neastward across the lower Mississippi Valley region as we finish \nup the week and go into next week. This will lead to a \ncontinuation of dry weather with large diurnal temperature ranges \napproaching 40 deg F.\n\nOf greatest importance, the continued drying of vegetation \ncombined with exceptionally low daytime RH values will help to \nmaintain an elevated fire danger threat. We will continue to \nmessage this through the graphics and fire danger statement. Wind \nshould have less influence and be a smaller factor in determining \nthe fire danger threat levels.\n\nOtherwise, rainfall is not expected until the end of next week at\nthe earliest, when the ridge shows signs of breaking down, and \nwhen the subtropical stream influence increases over our region.\nEven then, global ensembles show the greater rainfall signal \ncentered more to our west, so confidence is not great for this dry\nspell to end anytime soon. /EC/OAJ/",
